          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I am 68 with COPD and I have been more breathless for three days with green sputum. Is this an emergency, and what does guidance describe about exacerbations?",
          "answer": "You should seek medical advice regarding your increased breathlessness and green sputum. These symptoms may indicate an exacerbation of your COPD, which, while not an immediate emergency, does warrant attention from your clinician. The guidance indicates that if symptoms worsen or become new and unexplained, it is essential to consult a healthcare provider (source: General clinical reference - \"Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)\").\n\nExacerbations are acute worsenings of respiratory symptoms and can frequently be triggered by infections (source: General clinical reference on COPD). It’s important to monitor your symptoms and consult your clinician to determine any necessary adjustments to your management plan. Each exacerbation can negatively impact your quality of life, accelerate lung function decline, and can predict future issues (source: Global respiratory strategy report - \"COPD: spirometric diagnosis, group assessment and initial therapy\").\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
